WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.360
Hey folks! Welcome to my weird background&nbsp;
commanders deckbuilding challenge. Two weeks ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.560
I gave the following prompt to my discord server:&nbsp;
build a deck using one of the choose-a-background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.160
commanders you see on screen now, partnered with&nbsp;
a background of the person’s choosing. And, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:18.040
were a lot of decks this time around—doing a&nbsp;
first pass-through by myself worked fine last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:23.160
time so I figured I’d do that again this&nbsp;
time, and, uh… it took awhile. It’s fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.560
I had a lovely time looking through 400 decks.&nbsp;
The judging criteria were creativity, cohesion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.120
and likelihood to produce dynamic gameplay. The&nbsp;
first two of those were the main two—I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.320
see decks that were cool, interesting, and unique&nbsp;
in some way, and I wanted decks where the command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.880
zone and all other major elements of the deck&nbsp;
felt cohesively tied together into a unified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.040
and functioning package. There’s nothing majorly&nbsp;
wrong with a cohesive deck that’s a little bland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.240
or a funky deck that’s a little all over the&nbsp;
place, but in this challenge I was looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.680
the confluence between these two features. After&nbsp;
I had done a first passthrough on the decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.640
I discussed the ones that stood out to me&nbsp;
the most with my cohost on the Magic Mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.280
Podcast 3/3 Elk and my IRL friend Hans,&nbsp;
and picked out a winner for each of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.280
five commanders based on their input. So,&nbsp;
this video will be showcasing those decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.840
and touching briefly on honorable mentions for&nbsp;
each commander as well. Those honorable mentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.360
will be featured in their own tab on my website,&nbsp;
linked in the description. Also, I’ll be showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.840
some fun charts for these commanders as well,&nbsp;
compiled by people in my discord server. Shout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.200
outs to Jack and A Garbage Bag of Human Teeth&nbsp;
for going to the effort of putting that together.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.240
Without further ado, the first commander option&nbsp;
was Alora, Merry Thief, a 3 mana 3/2 with the text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.200
“Whenever you attack, up to one target attacking&nbsp;
creature can’t be blocked this turn. Return that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.400
creature to its owner’s hand at the beginning of&nbsp;
the next end step. This is an interesting card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.600
because bouncing a creature at end of turn&nbsp;
is generally a significant downside for most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.280
decks and not worth the benefit of giving that&nbsp;
creature unblockable. This commander had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.320
most submissions at around 100, and people tackled&nbsp;
this in a variety of different ways—some used Ball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:02.840
Lightning style cards that would otherwise sac at&nbsp;
end of turn, some used ninjas, a lot used Alora as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.880
a way to recycle ETBs, some initiative decks, and&nbsp;
a couple paired Alora with Cultist of the Absolute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.440
and some haste equipments in order to avoid&nbsp;
needing to sacrifice a creature on upkeep. As you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.040
can see on this plot, Izzet Alora decks were most&nbsp;
popular, with Dimir Alora decks falling closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.680
behind. For a few honorable mentions, we have&nbsp;
a deck based around the card Dawn of the Dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.640
a deck that focuses on the card Aether vial&nbsp;
to help with getting creatures back into play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.920
and a spell-less spellslinger deck—that one is a&nbsp;
funny one and it was a favorite among people on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.720
discord server. For the winning deck, though,&nbsp;
we have a returning winner—Filthystaxplayer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.520
submitted a Sneak Attack deck, with Guild&nbsp;
Artisan chosen as the partnered background.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:46.760
Now, the choice to run Sneak Attack wasn’t a&nbsp;
unique one for this challenge—getting creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.120
out at a discount to be sacrificed at end of&nbsp;
turn makes sense as a way to utilize Alora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.440
as was a reasonably popular choice as a&nbsp;
result. However, this deck had a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.520
of construction choices that made it stand&nbsp;
out above the rest of the crowd. For one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.760
its utilization of its background—Guild&nbsp;
artisan wasn’t a widely popular background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.960
in the challenge, but it can be a nice&nbsp;
treasure engine. More importantly, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.560
this deck pairs it with polymorph effects—that’s&nbsp;
right, this deck can tutor out a Sneak Attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.720
effect via one of two effects that remove&nbsp;
a permanent to grab one of the same type,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.920
doing so by polymorphing its own background which&nbsp;
is conveniently castable from the command zone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.960
This can also work with the treasures produced&nbsp;
by Guild Artisan to polymorph for artifacts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:30.840
since Chaotic Transformation can target multiple&nbsp;
permanent types and Reality Scramble can be cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.760
multiple times via Retrace. These are supplemented&nbsp;
with a handful of tutors that can find instants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.440
and sorceries, enabling the deck to have&nbsp;
consistent access to its key enchantments.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:45.280
One natural question for a Sneak Attack deck&nbsp;
is “what is being snuck out?” In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:51.000
there’s a total of 16 creatures of mana value 6&nbsp;
or greater, a pretty robust number. Among these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.040
some number are your typical cheat deck beaters&nbsp;
such as Ancient Stone Idol and Cityscape Leveler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.600
but past these there are some more interesting&nbsp;
ones. Balefire Dragon provides a source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.400
large-scale removal, Knollspine Dragon can allow&nbsp;
for some big draw with creative turn ordering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.000
and there are also a number of ETB&nbsp;
creatures that can get value turn over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.000
turn with Sneak Attack, including&nbsp;
Tyrant of Discord, Astral Dragon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.080
and Trumpeting Carnosaur. There’s also&nbsp;
another key top end creature, Nezahal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.400
and that one can serve as a gesture to a key&nbsp;
subtheme within this deck—flicker. Flicker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:30.200
cards perform two functions here—as with the&nbsp;
commander, they can recycle ETB creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:34.560
and they can also flicker a creature put out with&nbsp;
Sneak Attack to keep it around permanently at no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.240
additional expense. This gives the deck a great&nbsp;
degree of flexibility in how hard it wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.120
commit to the board, and in how much it wants&nbsp;
to grind value. There’s also a good amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:48.800
redundancy of key types of effect—there are four&nbsp;
total cards that can perform a Sneak Attack like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.280
role, two enchantments and two non-enchantment&nbsp;
cards that are similar enough, and there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.240
likewise multiple alternative options for&nbsp;
keeping around Sneak Attacked creatures outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:02.120
of the command zone. Overall, this is a super&nbsp;
cool deck—great job, again, filthystaxplayer!

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:09.200
Next up, we have Amber Gristle O’Maul, a&nbsp;
4 mana 3/3 with haste and “Whenever this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.840
creature attacks, you may discard your hand.&nbsp;
If you do, draw a card for each player being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.720
attacked.” Naturally, this encourages&nbsp;
a lot of low slung, scrappy strategies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:22.120
and this was a commander where an impressive&nbsp;
portion of the decks were low budget decks. All of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:28.240
my top picks here were less than $150, some a lot&nbsp;
less than that. As you can see from the plot here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.000
black was a very dominant choice for&nbsp;
a background color among Amber decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.800
which makes sense given her self-discard&nbsp;
oriented gameplay. For honorable mentions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:42.680
we’ve got a pump spells voltron deck, a budget&nbsp;
aggro deck, and a reanimator deck. For the #1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.800
amber deck, though, we’ve got Lord Reverend’s&nbsp;
clerics and dwarves deck, with Haunted One as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:52.800
the partnered background. Really, this deck&nbsp;
focuses on one specific variety of creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.560
that being clerics and dwarves that sacrifice&nbsp;
creatures to accomplish tasks. We’ve got 11 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:04.760
these, along with 12 other clerics and 5 other&nbsp;
dwarves. The basic idea here seems simple—dump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:09.320
these creatures into play alongside Amber and&nbsp;
Haunted One, and then swing in with Amber in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:15.120
order to both wheel and use sacrifice abilities&nbsp;
on the now-undying clerics and dwarves. This can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.760
produce effects such as destroying a creature&nbsp;
with Blind Zealot, tutoring out black creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:25.000
with Shadow-rite Priest, and drawing cards with&nbsp;
Skullport Merchant. The deck also contains five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.720
cards that reanimate in some way, including three&nbsp;
attached to a cleric, as well as three madness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:34.120
cards, so there’s some degree of synergy present&nbsp;
to work with Amber’s attack trigger. Her power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:39.480
and toughness aren’t great—only a 3/3 on a 4 mana&nbsp;
creature—but this doesn’t matter as long as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:44.520
deck has Haunted One in play. This might prompt a&nbsp;
followup question—what if you want to sacrifice a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.400
creature that already has a +1/+1 counter on it?&nbsp;
This solution to this potential issue comes in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:54.240
form of a handful of cards that remove counters&nbsp;
from creatures. There are nine of these total,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.560
and while most of these feel more like filler&nbsp;
than anything else, the two enchantments seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.240
great. Ion Storm provides a quality source&nbsp;
of repeatable burn damage, and Retribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:07.880
of the Ancients is an even more potent tool,&nbsp;
particularly when the deck’s board gets bigger.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.640
The other standout element of this deck is&nbsp;
its suite of boardwipes. The deck runs an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:17.960
eye-watering nine boardwipes, which the deck can&nbsp;
break parity on with through Haunted One. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:22.240
these nine is Jokulhaups, which can provide a&nbsp;
game-warping nuke once the pilot of this deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:27.000
trusts their board to run to the finish line.&nbsp;
These board wipes seem like an appropriate tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:31.640
to help the deck’s honestly mediocre creatures&nbsp;
get around the large boards of modern-day EDH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:38.880
decks. Because that’s the most palpable vibe this&nbsp;
deck gives off—the jank factor. The cards here are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:44.000
at best normally mediocre cards being used in&nbsp;
their narrow use-case window of being decent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:50.560
and in some cases mediocre cards even in this&nbsp;
deck. We’ve got late 90s dwarves, various mediocre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.400
commons from across the years, Grimoire&nbsp;
of the Dead—there’s so much nonsense here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.400 --> 00:08:01.320
united in flavor or function or both. This feels&nbsp;
appropriate, though, for a few reasons. Amber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:05.560
is a fundamentally janky commander—she’s a draw&nbsp;
engine, but one that requires playing in a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:11.800
scrappy style, and who doesn’t even reward that&nbsp;
style that heavily. Her two creature types—Cleric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:16.680
and Dwarf—are not generally great ones to build&nbsp;
around. Clerics have gotten just enough love to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:22.120
make Shadowborn Apostle decks look a quarter&nbsp;
of the way respectable, but dwarves? Yeah no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:27.880
forget about it. In addition, this deck’s budget&nbsp;
is quite low—it’s sitting below the $50 mark if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:33.240
you exclude Haunted One, a card which, as Lord&nbsp;
Reverend alluded to, is basically free as long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:37.960
as you win this competition, since the prize is an&nbsp;
altered art version of the card. A wildly polished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:43.240
deck with top tier card quality this certainly is&nbsp;
not, nor was it intended to be. As a final note,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:48.080
this deck also has an undeniable thematic core&nbsp;
to it. We have themes of death and dying riddled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.320
throughout this deck, including the Haunted One&nbsp;
background, a whole lot of cultist-type creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.600
that sacrifice things to perform rituals, and&nbsp;
board wipes that represent great dying-off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:02.400
events. Lore-wise, Amber herself is a cleric with&nbsp;
connections to the Shadowfell, carrying a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.640
degree of liminality with her, tugged between life&nbsp;
and what lies beyond. This further helps the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:12.320
in communicating itself as a cohesive unit, and&nbsp;
the end result has an undeniable sense of panache.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:18.320
Next, let’s talk about our white commander&nbsp;
option, Ellyn Harbeeze Busybody. She’s a 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:23.200
mana 2/4 creature with “Tap: look at the top X&nbsp;
cards of your library, where X is the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.120
tokens you created this turn. Put one of those&nbsp;
into your hand and the rest on the bottom of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.960
your library in any order.” People took this&nbsp;
base in a variety of different directions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:37.440
but there were two main patterns. One were decks&nbsp;
that create tokens and also really want a source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.640
of filtering, an example of which might be a combo&nbsp;
deck or some sort of synergy hungry machine sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.840
of deck. The other type of deck people built were&nbsp;
decks more specifically interested in maximizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:53.000
the level of dig they could get out of Ellyn,&nbsp;
either through creating a lot of tokens or by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:58.520
untapping her. As you can see from the pie chart,&nbsp;
most of the decks featured either black or green,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:04.080
though this was not the case for our honorable&nbsp;
mentions here. These were three blue white decks:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.840
a deck built around flyers and the card&nbsp;
Mirrorweave, a hatebears deck with a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:13.560
of draw-denial effects, and a twiddle-storm deck&nbsp;
built around cards like Talrand, Sky Summoner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:19.520
However, winning deck was a different sort of&nbsp;
twiddlestorm deck—atrus159’s Bellringer deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:25.560
with Agent of the Iron Throne as the background.&nbsp;
This is a deck focused on effects that untap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:30.200
all of your creatures—4 of these untap&nbsp;
all creatures on opposing untap steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:35.960
8 untap all creatures once, and a few other cards&nbsp;
untap creatures more selectively. To go along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:41.440
these, this deck has 8 creatures that tap to&nbsp;
create a token, 7 cards that tap to add mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:46.400
and 2 cards that tap to create treasures,&nbsp;
effectively doing both of these things.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:50.200
Once this deck has Ellyn and some of these&nbsp;
effects online, it can start to do some cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:54.720
stuff. The deck can use all tap abilities,&nbsp;
creating mana and tokens and filtering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:58.960
and then it can play a card that untaps all&nbsp;
creatures to refresh all these tappers. Mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.280
creators let the deck avoid running out of&nbsp;
mana too fast, and creating tokens allows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:08.040
deck to dig deeper and deeper with Ellyn on each&nbsp;
round of tapping, hopefully finding additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:12.960
untapping effects for as long as the Ellyn&nbsp;
player wants to keep going. The deck doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:18.880
have an explicit wincon in the 98 outside of maybe&nbsp;
Halo Fountain, but the elegant thing here is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:24.720
the deck doesn’t really need a specific payoff&nbsp;
card for a big tapping and untapping turn. Agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:29.360
of the Iron Throne in the command zone means&nbsp;
that a board full of tokens already contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:31.760
an implied level of damage unless opponents&nbsp;
order their removal just right, and several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:37.440
Ellyn activations means a well-curated hand of&nbsp;
cards when passing after a big turn. To go along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:41.640
with the rest of this gameplan, this deck also&nbsp;
contains a handful of one-time token producers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:45.280
some protection for the commander, and&nbsp;
a small suite of other support cards.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.240
What’s interesting about this deck is that I&nbsp;
think both Agent of the Iron Throne and Haunted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:53.360
One work well as backgrounds, though they lend&nbsp;
very different play patterns for finishing off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:58.440
opponents. Agent of the Iron Throne gives the deck&nbsp;
a more measured playstyle that focuses more on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:02.560
optimizing twiddle turns in the moment and&nbsp;
worries about winning the game later once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:07.440
a whole lot of tokens are in play. Haunted&nbsp;
One, on the other hand, is more explosive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.760
rewarding careful planning and avoiding opposing&nbsp;
hate with the potential for some truly disgusting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:17.320
alpha strikes. Either way seems valid enough to&nbsp;
me, though something nice about Haunted One is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:22.280
that it also gives the deck a bit of something&nbsp;
that it’s a bit low on at the moment—protection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:27.120
Ellyn can tap herself at any time she’s untapped&nbsp;
to grant the benefit of Haunted One, even if no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.800
tokens were created during a turn, which gives&nbsp;
a pretty sweet tool for dealing with removal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:36.800
This is relevant because deck’s strategy—and&nbsp;
its access to a blood artist effect—depend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:41.240
on Ellyn being in play, and Ellyn getting&nbsp;
single-target removed can pose a meaningful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:46.120
setback for the deck. This issue is somewhat&nbsp;
counterbalanced by this deck’s high density&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:51.280
of ramp and inclusion of recovery cards such&nbsp;
as Dazzling Theater and Wand of the Worldsoul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:55.360
which can help the deck in getting Ellyn back&nbsp;
out on the board rather than stalling out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:59.520
so this more an idea to play around with&nbsp;
than any kind of major critique. Overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:04.520
this deck is well-built, dynamic,&nbsp;
and unique—great job atrus159!

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:10.280
Next up, let’s talk about Viconia, Drow Apostate.&nbsp;
She’s a 3 mana 2/3 whose text reads “At the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:14.080
beginning of your upkeep, if there are four or&nbsp;
more creature cards in your graveyard, return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:18.640
a creature card at random from your graveyard to&nbsp;
your hand.” On the surface, this card is similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:24.120
to an enchantment called Oversold Cemetery,&nbsp;
with one key distinction—the returned creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:28.800
is chosen at random. This immediately causes&nbsp;
some problems with the use cases of the card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:32.600
because if you throw a bunch of filler creatures&nbsp;
into your graveyard plus a desirable key creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:36.680
or two, you’re probably gonna get back garbage&nbsp;
off of Viconia, or at least that’s the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:41.440
likely outcome per trigger. Naturally, there were&nbsp;
a mixture of different solutions players found to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:46.640
this issue—some chose to have most creatures be&nbsp;
functionally identical, or chose synergy packages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:50.840
where getting back any creature was beneficial&nbsp;
regardless of what it was. From this chart you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:55.560
can see that colors were more spread out with&nbsp;
Viconia than with most of the others, though green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.560 --> 00:14:00.440
was still the most popular. For a few honorable&nbsp;
mentions, there was an Umori companion-ed Golgari&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:05.840
midrange deck, a modular creatures deck, and&nbsp;
an Astral Slide deck. For the winning deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:11.560
we have a different cycling deck—Chimes’ high&nbsp;
mana value cycling deck, with Tavern Brawler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:16.320
as the background commander. This was the one&nbsp;
winning deck that 3/3 Elk dissented on, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:21.640
they did note that from a lore standpoint having&nbsp;
Viconia drunkenly fighting people is very funny.

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:25.320
As you might guess from the description of the&nbsp;
deck I gave, the foundation of this deck lies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:30.800
upon one simple fact—there are a lot of 5-6&nbsp;
mana creatures with some form of cycling or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:37.240
other. This deck contains 13 each of generic&nbsp;
cycling creatures and landcycling creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:42.040
plus an additional three channel creatures which&nbsp;
can be discarded for an effect. This means the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:46.000
deck has a pretty easy time getting creatures in&nbsp;
the graveyard for purposes of getting Viconia’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:51.000
ability online. In addition, all of these&nbsp;
landcycling creatures can basically exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:55.160
in place of a land, so that significantly&nbsp;
lowers the number of lands that need to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.160 --> 00:15:00.760
in the deck. The other thing that can help with&nbsp;
this is MDFC lands, at least in the sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:06.000
they allow the deck to contain more cards whose&nbsp;
front side is not a land. This is important for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:11.080
purposes of Tavern Brawler, since hitting a&nbsp;
land with the card doesn’t buff Viconia. As a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:16.840
result of its 13 landcycling creatures and its&nbsp;
11 MDFC lands, this deck’s official landcount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:23.520
excluding MDFCs, is 14. My manabase tool does&nbsp;
rate this deck’s manabase as “very strong”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:28.080
after plugging in some appropriate discounts,&nbsp;
and I’m inclined to agree with that analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:32.400
though the caveat with that is that there will&nbsp;
be lands entering tapped and turns spending mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:36.440
landcycling. The end product of this is that&nbsp;
this deck has an eye-wateringly high average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:42.360
card cost of 4.3, and that’s including lands!&nbsp;
This means that the deck is likely going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:46.920
able to push a decent amount of damage with a&nbsp;
Tavern Brawling Viconia. There are a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:50.880
evasion cards to help with this, as well as a&nbsp;
decent suite of removal to clear out blockers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:55.640
or push an opponent into unfavorable blocks. The&nbsp;
other thing accomplished by the command zone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:59.800
that it contains two different value engines,&nbsp;
which can allow the deck to maintain a solid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:04.680
baseline of gameplay volume, even if there are&nbsp;
some setbacks. Finally, the deck has a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:09.040
other cards which can take advantage of its high&nbsp;
average mana value, in addition to Tavern Brawler.

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:14.120
As it stands now, this deck is a funky midrange&nbsp;
battlecruiser brew with some fun side paths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:19.560
However, more so than being a perfectly conceived&nbsp;
final deck, I like this as a deck that has a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:24.680
of good ideas and comfortably dips its toes in a&nbsp;
lot of different archetypes, a deck whose elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:29.120
gesture to a variety of different directions&nbsp;
that it could be further honed. More ramp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:33.200
could amplify the battlecruiser side of it, more&nbsp;
reanimation spells could amplify the explosive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:37.520
creature-cheating side of it, more evasion&nbsp;
could amplify the voltron elements of this deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:42.160
more discard or other resource-attacking methods&nbsp;
could amplify the grindy aspects of this deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:46.960
and so on. It’s also very budget at the moment,&nbsp;
and could either be kept that way or upgraded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:50.240
with a handful of more powerful cards. Good&nbsp;
stuff. Thanks for the awesome brew, Chimes!

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:55.720
Finally, let’s talk about our green commander&nbsp;
option. Halsin Emerald Archdruid is a four mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.720 --> 00:17:01.960
2/4 with the ability to pay one mana to turn a&nbsp;
token into a 4/4 green bear until end of turn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:06.400
This is a great ability in terms of raw rate,&nbsp;
like those tokens were probably something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:13.040
base power 1/1 if they were creatures so that’s a&nbsp;
+3/+3 for one mana, and if they weren’t creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:18.360
before that’s a whole different degree of value.&nbsp;
However, despite having a lot of potential, Halsin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:24.080
also presents a handful of questions. What tokens&nbsp;
should he be animating? And, what should be done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:29.000
with a bunch of 4/4s? There were some creative&nbsp;
answers to these questions among the submissions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:34.400
As you can see on this plot, blue and white were&nbsp;
quite dominant as second colors, constituting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:39.160
around a third of decks each. For a few honorable&nbsp;
mentions, there was a deck that animated aura&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:44.920
tokens to generate sac triggers off of Martyr’s&nbsp;
Bond, a powerstones deck, and a tokens enchantress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:49.600
deck that used Opalescence to get an additional&nbsp;
copy of the background bonus. God Opalescence has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:50.527
good art, can we talk about that? John Avon really&nbsp;
knocked it out of the park with that one. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.527 --> 00:17:55.000
for the winning deck, though, we have CubiCCL’s&nbsp;
incubate deck, with Inspiring Leader used as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.000 --> 00:18:00.200
a background. Incubate is an unusual mechanic&nbsp;
introduced in March of the Machine that creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:05.320
artifact tokens with +1/+1 counters on them, and&nbsp;
these tokens can later be transformed into 0/0&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:11.400
creatures for two mana. This has some very clear&nbsp;
upsides paired with Halsin’s ability—for one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:16.000
we get all the benefits of the +1/+1 counters on&nbsp;
the tokens without needing to pay the two mana to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:22.440
flip them, instead paying 1 mana to get them for&nbsp;
a turn with an additional +4/+4 on top. Secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:27.320
the fact that these tokens don’t need to be&nbsp;
flipped makes them more resilient—instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:32.640
exploding to either an artifact wipe or a creature&nbsp;
wipe, these tokens will generally only die to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:37.560
artifact wipes since they’re typically not&nbsp;
creatures on our opponents’ turns. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:42.160
also don’t even have to be creatures on your&nbsp;
turn—this deck runs a number of board wipes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:45.880
which have the potential to be kinda one-sided&nbsp;
with the deck storing power and toughness.

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:52.200
The deck contains 12 total cards that create&nbsp;
incubator tokens, and with their relative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:57.160
permanence in mind it also runs a number of cards&nbsp;
that can proliferate the +1/+1 counters on them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:02.080
gradually ramping up the size of these tokens&nbsp;
turn over turn. There are also 8 other cards in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:05.960
the deck that use counters in some way, which is&nbsp;
good as it means that the proliferate cards are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:11.040
going to be getting value outside of incubators,&nbsp;
of which there aren’t that many due to them only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:16.120
being in one set. Likewise, many of these cards&nbsp;
that use counters also create tokens, with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:21.560
deck containing a total of 16 other token&nbsp;
creating cards outside of the incubate cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:25.680
which is good as the deck’s main plan for winning&nbsp;
is animating things with Halsin and swinging in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:32.440
with them. The background is here to amplify that&nbsp;
plan, boosting the 4/4 bears up to 6/6 bears. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:36.560
buff might be less meaningful in contexts with&nbsp;
an especially large number of tokens, but when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:40.960
the deck only has a handful of tokens to swing in&nbsp;
with, that size difference can be quite relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:46.360
to the outcome of the combat. As I alluded&nbsp;
to earlier, this deck has seven board wipes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:50.760
which further enhance its combat potential. While&nbsp;
the commander isn’t likely to survive a wipe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:55.840
the incubate tokens usually will, which can allow&nbsp;
for a nice swing on a following turn, or I suppose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.840 --> 00:20:00.360
the same turn if the deck has a lot of mana.&nbsp;
Overall, this deck is a well-rounded midrange deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:05.560
based around a not-very-heavily-played mechanic&nbsp;
that goes excellently with Halsin. Love to see it!

00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:09.320
Alright, those were the winning decks.&nbsp;
I appreciate everybody who participated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:12.600
as even though it took a while to go through&nbsp;
the submissions, it’s always super fun to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:15.920
what people come up with. You can check out&nbsp;
the honorable mentions in the link down in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:19.720
the description, and you can also feel free to&nbsp;
let me know in the comments what you thought of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:24.000
these decks. What was your favorite? Was there&nbsp;
a deck that I didn’t pick that you think was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:28.800
terribly robbed? You can also let me know if you&nbsp;
have ideas for future deckbuilding challenges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:33.600
because these are fun and I’ll probably do&nbsp;
2-3 more later this year. With that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:37.520
thanks for watching! And thanks to my Patrons&nbsp;
for supporting my work on this channel. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:41.440
want to join them, a link to my Patreon is down&nbsp;
in the description, and I greatly appreciate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:45.720
generosity of all those who contribute.&nbsp;
Alright, that’s all for this one, bye.

